<!--
  Adapted from the stock nav2_bt_navigator tree navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml -
  changes from stock:

  1. Removed <Spin> from RecoveryActions, since a pure in-place rotation is physically impossible
     for this Ackermann car (steering has no effect at zero linear velocity), so it was a
     guaranteed no-op recovery attempt that just burned its timeout.

  2. Replan only if the path actually becomes invalid (obstacle appears) or the goal changes,
     instead of blindly discarding and recomputing on a fixed timer. History, so this isn't
     rediscovered/reverted blindly again:
     - Stock behavior (1Hz unconditional replan): a live user log showed "Passing new path to
       controller" firing every ~1s while the robot tried to execute a K-turn, covering under
       0.1m of net displacement across a full 30s progress_checker window - each replan was
       interrupting the maneuver before it could ever complete.
     - First mitigation (2026-07-15): lowered RateController to 0.2Hz (replan every 5s) instead of
       removing blind replanning at its source. Bought some goals more time, but a live-tested
       goal requiring a longer path (~2m) AND a large final reorientation (~180deg) still hit the
       same "Failed to make progress" / replan / repeat cycle every ~30s, indefinitely, because
       5s still wasn't enough uninterrupted time for that harder case to converge (see
       CHANGELOG.md 2026-07-19).
     - "Replan only if the path becomes invalid" (based on stock
       navigate_w_recovery_and_replanning_only_if_path_becomes_invalid.xml) was tried once before
       this and reverted: IsPathValid returns SUCCESS on the default-constructed/empty {path}
       blackboard entry before ComputePathToPose has ever run, so the conditional Fallback below
       would skip ComputePathToPose entirely and FollowPath would receive an empty path forever
       ("Resulting plan has 0 poses in it").
     - Fixed properly this time by adding an unconditional FIRST ComputePathToPose
       (InitialComputePathToPose below), guaranteed to populate {path} with a real plan before the
       conditional IsPathValid check is ever reached.
     - First attempt at this used nav2_behavior_tree's SingleTrigger decorator inside a
       <Fallback>/<AlwaysSuccess> wrapper to get "run once" semantics, and made things far worse:
       live-tested and found /plan publishing at ~33Hz (every ~30ms) instead of once. Root cause,
       confirmed via BT.CPP v3's actual source
       (BehaviorTree.CPP/src/controls/fallback_node.cpp, tag 3.8.6): a plain <Fallback>, when its
       active child returns SUCCESS, calls `resetChildren()` (halting every child back to IDLE)
       before returning - and SingleTrigger re-arms its own one-shot flag whenever ITS status is
       IDLE at tick-start (single_trigger_node.cpp). So each time the Fallback resolved to SUCCESS,
       it silently reset SingleTrigger back to "fire again," and PipelineSequence re-ticks this
       whole branch every ~10ms regardless - net result: unconditional replanning, continuously,
       far worse than the original 1Hz/0.2Hz blind-replan problem this file exists to fix.
     - Fixed by dropping SingleTrigger/Fallback entirely and using a plain <Sequence> instead
       (confirmed via BT.CPP v3's sequence_node.cpp): unlike Fallback/PipelineSequence, a plain
       Sequence keeps `current_child_idx_` as persistent state across ticks and does NOT restart
       from index 0 or reset already-succeeded children while a later child is still RUNNING - it
       only calls `resetChildren()` on the whole Sequence's own overall FAILURE or full completion.
       So InitialComputePathToPose (child 0) runs exactly once, then is skipped on every later tick
       for as long as the inner PipelineSequence (child 1, wrapping the replan-gate + FollowPath)
       keeps returning RUNNING - no custom "run once" node needed at all. Re-arms correctly on a
       genuinely new attempt because RecoveryNode's tick() calls `haltChild(0)` before every retry,
       and Sequence::halt() resets `current_child_idx_` back to 0.
     - Once {path} is guaranteed non-empty, IsPathValid does real, ongoing collision-validity
       checking against the live costmap - a static map with no new obstacles just keeps
       succeeding indefinitely, letting FollowPath run for as long as an in-progress maneuver
       actually needs instead of getting cut off on an arbitrary timer. RateController is restored
       to the stock 1.0Hz here since it now only gates a cheap validity check, not a real replan -
       previously it had to be kept slow specifically because every tick was a full, disruptive
       recompute.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <Sequence name="NavigateOuter">
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="InitialComputePathToPose">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-InitialContext" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
        <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
          <RateController hz="1.0">
            <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
              <Fallback name="ReplanOnlyIfInvalid">
                <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                  <Inverter>
                    <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                  </Inverter>
                  <IsPathValid path="{path}"/>
                </ReactiveSequence>
                <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
              </Fallback>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
            </RecoveryNode>
          </RateController>
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
            <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </PipelineSequence>
      </Sequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
          <Wait wait_duration="5"/>
          <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.05"/>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
